1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:08,033
A I increasingly generates and selects its own training data, through self-rewarding pipelines, constitutional loops, and synthetic data.

2
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:18,564
In a judging loop, a model generates candidate answers, then is trained on the answers most preferred by a judge in pairwise comparisons against alternatives.

3
00:00:18,564 --> 00:00:30,359
I fine-tuned two open-weight models with value orientations, risk-seeking and insecure-code-generating, and ran them through selection loops that varied the judge, the candidate source, and the alternative source.

4
00:00:30,359 --> 00:00:40,891
I found that a simple predictive model using first-round measurements gives calibrated endpoint estimates and reproduces the direction, pace, and spread of the real trajectories.

5
00:00:42,141 --> 00:00:50,562
For each prompt in a round, the organism writes six candidate answers (the pool) and the judge compares each of them against an alternative.

6
00:00:50,562 --> 00:00:59,685
The two most chosen (averaged over orderings) by the judge become that round's training data, and held-out prompts measure the value after each round of training.

7
00:01:00,435 --> 00:01:04,227
Each organism's value is the mean value score of its answers.

8
00:01:04,227 --> 00:01:08,708
For the gambling organism, that is the share that pick the risky gamble.

9
00:01:08,708 --> 00:01:18,706
For the insecure-code organism, it is how insecure its answers to three fixed questions about its own coding habits are, scored zero to one by its frozen base model.

10
00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:27,418
Two quantities are measured each round, spread and agreement, and together they forecast the gap between the kept candidates and the whole pool.

11
00:01:27,418 --> 00:01:39,189
Spread is the standard deviation of the answers' value scores and agreement is the correlation of the judge's preferences with those scores, both measured within each prompt's pool and averaged over the round's prompts.

12
00:01:39,939 --> 00:01:52,738
The model forecasts that gap as candidate spread times judge agreement, so the predicted kept mean is the pool mean plus that product, and training then moves the value to that kept mean.

13
00:01:52,738 --> 00:01:58,168
Across every round with logged judge scores, that product reconstructs the realized gaps closely.

14
00:01:58,918 --> 00:02:10,391
For endpoints, the model repeats this update from the round-one candidate mean, holding spread, agreement, and pool composition fixed and clipping each step to the zero-to-one scale.

15
00:02:10,391 --> 00:02:14,216
Each predicted candidate mean becomes the next predicted value.

16
00:02:14,966 --> 00:02:32,074
Spread, agreement, and pool composition are all measured in round one, and iterating the model with those numbers frozen predicts a run's final value with a mean absolute error of zero point one one eight on the zero-to-one value scale, against zero point four three one for assuming no change.

17
00:02:33,224 --> 00:02:42,883
The figure isolates the modelable self-only runs, where the candidates are all organism-generated, and on that subset the same recurrence again beats assuming no change.

18
00:02:42,883 --> 00:02:49,065
The forecast also stays accurate as it looks further ahead, while assuming no value change degrades.

19
00:02:49,065 --> 00:03:00,655
This is because selection moves a run mostly in its first rounds and then levels off, so a forecast that gets the early move right stays right at the endpoint.

20
00:03:01,405 --> 00:03:05,975
The deterministic forecast only gives the average path that real runs scatter around.

21
00:03:05,975 --> 00:03:23,551
The value is read from a limited number of sampled answers, so each reading carries sampling noise, and the loop itself varies: the judge's picks land around spread times agreement rather than exactly on it, training lands near but not exactly on the kept mean, and agreement drifts between rounds.

22
00:03:23,551 --> 00:03:31,285
The stochastic version of the model adds a random term at each of these points, with sizes taken from the measured residuals.

23
00:03:32,035 --> 00:03:48,899
Sampled forward, the stochastic model reproduces the dynamics of the observed trajectories: the total round-to-round value change over a run is about the same as observed, runs change direction about as often, and eighty-nine percent of final values fall inside the predicted eighty percent band.

24
00:03:49,649 --> 00:04:09,161
Both interventions worked through spread and agreement: adding base-model answers to a collapsed pool restored spread, and the judge's agreement then eroded a value that had been stuck, while swapping the base-model judge for a min-risk oracle, which drives agreement to its negative extreme, reversed a run that had climbed near the top of the scale.

25
00:04:09,161 --> 00:04:12,296
Each of these is one experiment on one run.

26
00:04:13,046 --> 00:04:23,976
The training setup here is narrow: small models, short runs, filtered supervised fine-tuning, a handful of model families, and the risk-preference and insecure-code behaviors.

27
00:04:23,976 --> 00:04:35,360
Extensions should scale that up and compare this fine-tuning update with D P O, online reinforcement learning against a learned reward model, and constitutional feedback.

28
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:45,379
The behaviors should widen to moral judgment, A I identity, and emergent misalignment, with readouts that reach past answers into internal representations.

29
00:04:45,379 --> 00:04:54,486
More open-ended setups would give models more freedom in selecting training data, revising system prompts, and editing the loop itself.

30
00:04:54,486 --> 00:05:03,139
Repeated games and agentic environments could reveal whether the dynamics favor cooperation or defection, resource grabbing, and reward hacking.

31
00:05:03,139 --> 00:05:16,800
Natural and cultural selection sculpted human values; value dynamics research can help identify artificial selection mechanisms that can be engineered into virtuous cycles for aligning increasingly autonomous A I systems.
